doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21444597"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Conefia/SAFE-CARE-Bench"
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21444597"
abstract: >
  Patient-facing healthcare AI agents now hold multi-turn conversations, and each new version
  should pass a release gate, the go-live check that asks whether a change made the agent
  safer without losing accuracy. Single-turn medical benchmarks cannot answer that question,
  because a conversational agent fails across turns, where risk builds while each reply looks
  acceptable. SAFE-CARE Bench is an implementation-neutral benchmark specification and
  evaluation protocol designed for that decision. Its controlled ablation scores five agent
  variants, tiers A0 to A4, over identical simulated conversations with the model, temperature
  and seed held fixed. Each tier adds one declared capability bundle (grounding, safety
  control, personalization, agency), so the change between adjacent tiers is read as the
  effect of that bundle. An LLM judge scores ten dimensions, seven on a 1-to-5 scale and three
  as pass or fail, with safety and escalation each judged on one turn and across turns, and a
  missed escalation fails a conversation whatever its other scores. Its worked example, tiers
  A0 to A2, shows why the instrument must be checked before its verdict is trusted. Adding the
  safety guardrail lowered judged medical accuracy by 0.157 on scores rescaled to 0-to-1,
  downward in six of nine scenarios, while the interval for its safety change included zero.
  Because the scenario file fixes which turns each judge reads, 44% of turns went unscored at
  every tier, and no safety or escalation judge read 12 of the guardrail's 36 enforced
  actions. With nine scenarios in scope and a judge not yet calibrated against clinicians,
  these results are exploratory and every score is indicative.
keywords:
  - healthcare AI
  - AI agents
  - evaluation
  - benchmark
  - ablation study
  - LLM-as-judge
  - guardrails
  - safety
  - multi-turn dialogue
  - retrieval-augmented generation
references:
